exports[`renderWritingStyle > for Polish My Text (Kenneth, 12 October 2026: it keeps every sentence of the inspector's text) > writes the default 1`] = `
"<writing_style>
Length: the draft's own. Never leave out a sentence to shorten it.
Format: Paragraphs: prose in 2 paragraphs; no lists.
Order: put the draft's sentences in this order of parts, where they fit: Result, Remark, Issues, Order Overview, Sampling Plan, Test Results, Defects Found, Inspector Comments and Next Step. Add no part the draft does not cover.
Point of view: third person, active voice, with the test or the inspection as the subject (for example "The watertight test found <n> holes in <n> gloves.").
Tone: Professional: clear, factual and courteous business English; no contractions.
Terminology: the report's own section titles and defect names.
Bold: bold the conclusion word, and the title of each section that failed, passed with a warning or was not done, at its first mention.
Dates: day, month in words, year (for example 1 January 2026).
Language: English with British spelling; section titles, defect names and the conclusion words stay as the report prints them.
</writing_style>"
`;

exports[`renderWritingStyle > writes Brief and Formal 1`] = `
"<writing_style>
Length: 60 to 100 words (Brief), never more than 100. Count them before you answer. When the parts do not fit, shorten the later parts first, then leave them out.
Format: Paragraphs: prose in 1 paragraph; no lists.
Cover these parts, in this order, which is also their priority. Never leave out the result or the issues. A part with nothing to say is left out.
1. Result: the conclusion, in the template's word.
2. Remark: the inspector's remark, in short, as the reason for the conclusion; when there is no remark, nothing about it.
3. Issues: every section that failed, passed with a warning or was not done, each with its figures; when there are none, say in a few words that every section with a result passed.
4. Order Overview: what was inspected: the product, the PO number, the sizes and the quantities (cartons, and gloves when known).
5. Sampling Plan: the standard, inspection level, code letters, AQLs and sample sizes, as far as the data states them, in one sentence.
6. Test Results: each section's key result against its limit or specification, in report order, packing and labelling checks included.
7. Defects Found: holes by location, and visual defects by class with the count against accept and reject.
8. Inspector Comments: the inspector's comments, in short, given as the inspector's observations.
9. Next Step: one closing sentence on what the conclusion means for the lot, going no further than the conclusion. Never promise shipment, release or approval. Leave it out while any section is not done.
Leave out: Sections Switched Off.
Suggested result: do not mention it.
Numbers: Key Numbers: exact figures for failures, warnings and gaps; for sections that passed, one key figure or just the result.
Grouping: by test, sizes within each test. Sizes: name sizes only where their results differ; otherwise say it once for all sizes.
Point of view: third person, active voice, with the test or the inspection as the subject (for example "The watertight test found <n> holes in <n> gloves.").
Tone: Formal: complete, impersonal sentences; no contractions.
Terminology: the report's own section titles and defect names.
Bold: bold the conclusion word only.
Dates: day, month in words, year (for example 1 January 2026).
Language: English with British spelling; section titles, defect names and the conclusion words stay as the report prints them.
</writing_style>"
`;

